Consider the following 3 schedules for 3 concurrent transactions T1, T2, T3

S1 = {r2(c), r2(b), w2(b), r3(b), r3(d), r3(c), r1(a), w1(a), w3(b), w3(c), r2(a), r2(d), w2(d), r1(b), w1(b), w2(a)}
S2 = {r3(b), r3(c), r3(d), r1(a), w1(a), w3(b), w3(c), r2(c), r1(b), w1(b), r2(b), w2(b), r2(a), w2(a), r2(d), w2(d)}
S3 = {r1(a), w1(a), r2(c), r2(b), w2(b), r2(d), r2(a), w2(a), w2(d), r1(b), w2(b), r3(b), r3(c), w3(b), w3(c), r3(d)}

For each of the three interleaved schedules, determine if the schedule is serializable. If so give an equivalent serial schedule.

Identify and justify how the following concurrency control problems affect data integrity and consistency in a multi-user environment.

S1:  r1(x);r2(x);w1(x);r3(x);w2(x);c2;r4(x);w3(x);c3; w1(y);c1;

S2: r1(x);w1(x);r2(x);w2(x);c2;r3(x);c3;w1(y);c1;

S3: r1(x); w1(x); r2(x); w2(x); r1(x);

Show the B+ tree that would be formed by inserting the month names (12 strings) into a tree in their normal calendar sequence (jan, feb, mar, apr, may, jun, jul, aug, sep, oct, nov, dec). After the construction, delete the entries for dec and mar.
